u.s troops are among the last to leave  among international forces and while  huge numbers of afghans have been flown  out many who want to leave are stranded  this and the looming humanitarian crisis  have set off a flurry of diplomatic  moves the u.s secretary of state anthony  blinken will hold a virtual meeting with  several allies including qatar and  turkey  while france and the uk will urge the  u.n security council to protect  civilians and ensure a safe passage for  those who want to escape  we have three correspondents covering  all these developments in afghanistan  kristen salumi is live at the united  nations to give us more details on  what's being discussed there for the  latest on the ground in kabul we have  rob mcbride standing by but first let's  take you to alan fisher he's at the  white house  to address that pentagon briefing so  very clear there the the message which  the pentagon is trying to emphasize is  that they've done  a huge job when it comes to evacuations  right alan  that's right and john kirby is saying  look no one could have expected the  afghan government to collapse quite so  quickly  there were people suggesting that that  might happen so there will be questions  after this about whether or not there  was a huge intelligence failure as there  was 20 years ago when osama bin laden  coordinated the attacks in the united  states on 9 11 which precipitated the us  going into afghanistan in the first  place  what was interesting of the five rockets  that were launched at the airport  overnight only one landed one was  intercepted by the rocket system three  landed outside the airport but this talk  about how they are able to continue to  target people even after the united  states uh pulls out is what they've  talked about for a long time this over  the horizon security capability to be  able to still neutralize threats that  they see  and i know from speaking to people over  the last few hours that the americans  really want this operation to be done  they want to get to the deadline they  want all the american troops out every  american who wants to leave they want  them out they want to remove as many  afghans as they possibly can and do it  without loss of life because as we've  seen  13 lives lost at the end of last week  and then a number of civilians killed  as the pentagon says it targeted what  was potentially a car bomb in kabul on  sunday they really just want to wake up  on wednesday or thursday morning and  have the headache of processing all  these people rather than have the  headache of ending  america's presence in afghanistan after  20 years and kind of related to that you  and i were chatting before the show  about the whole issue of counter  terrorism operations security  cooperation that came up in the press  briefing didn't it about  what sort of as the americans are  calling it over the horizon  counter-terrorism capabilities will be  maintained and how much coordination  will there be with the taliban  what is clear is that at the moment the  real politic dictates that the taliban's  in charge of afghanistan they're in  charge of kabul therefore the united  states has to work closely with them to  make sure that this operation continues  as smoothly as possible what happens in  the future well we have already heard  from the taliban who condemned the  attack uh that targeted  the so-called spotter the senior isil  figure that was killed in a drone attack  and then they also condemned the attack  on sunday which we believe may have  killed innocent civilians what is clear  from speaking to some security people i  know is that they don't think there will  be sharing of intelligence with the  taliban they say it's a complicated  situation but they believe that there is  too much overlap between isil k and the  taliban even though the two groups are  meant to be in opposition there are  still  links between the two groups and  therefore the united states are not  isn't going to flag anything up before  it carries out an operation if they  think in john kirby's words  that there is a a threat to the united  states or to americans emanating from  afghanistan then they will act  they said that although the us and the  the war in afghanistan  that's come to an end what will not end  is this commitment to continue to  protect americans so it will be  complicated  excuse me there may be discussions on a  diplomatic level between the taliban  and the united states but the idea of  them sharing intelligence uh according  to the people i've been speaking to  seems far-fetched unless  there is a common goal unless there is  something that the taliban  need dealt with or have highlighted and  the united states believes it's also in  its interest to take some action on that  front then there may be some joint  action but from the people i've been  speaking to say it's far too complicated  to consider sharing any intelligence  with the taliban all right good stuff  there from alan fisher let's thank him  for that let's head over now to rob  mcbride he joins us live from kabul and  of course we had those latest rocket  attacks  uh towards kabul airport what does the  situation on the ground look like or the  aftermath of those attacks look like  i think it is quite troubling for the  americans probably more in psychological  terms as they're now into this end game  of this evacuation exercise because  there seems to be little indication of  actual harm or damage but the u.s had  warned specifically about a rocket  attack as one of the continuing threats  from this islamic state affiliate it  seems to be prepared for that it  deployed as we just heard in  confirmation there with more details  from the pentagon this anti-rocket  system that it has it said it was  successful intercepting uh several of  the rockets as they came in as saying  that just one of those rockets fell  harmlessly it says uh in a an open piece  of land the uh  the um islamic s