 us departs they take with them  air traffic control and the taliban will  have the uh the unenviable job of not  only restoring airport but restoring  that air traffic control so it does  offer a way forward which everybody here  it seems wants to see the government the  aid organizations and most importantly  of course the population because that  would then  mean the return eventually of commercial  flights all right thanks so much rob  mcbride let's take this now to kristen  salumi she's joining us from the united  nations have been discussions there  about afghanistan where does security  council stand on getting a resolution  out  well we know that the united kingdom and  france have been pushing a resolution  that we expect some action on later in  the day  that essentially calls for the continued  safe passage of civilians and aid  agencies in and out of kabul  worth noting that the taliban has  said that civilians will be able to come  and go as they please  this essentially puts it in writing lets  the taliban know that the international  community plans to hold them to that  commitment by enshrining it in  international law the resolution also i  think attempts to put the focus back on  the humanitarian situation in the  country with so much attention on people  trying to leave afghanistan u.n  officials have been warning that the  situation on the ground is increasingly  dire there was concern about medical  supplies running out  earlier today who the world health  organization got its first flight into  the country  since the taliban takeover bringing much  needed medical supplies and health  related supplies into the country some  12 and a half metric tons that are uh  expected to be delivered to  29 different provinces and health  centers but that flight had to land in  uh mazzar e sharif it could not land in  kabul because of the terrible security  situation there  so un officials are  warning that there are some three and a  half million  displaced 